E=[
(1,1884,"Falls","Black rain in the Clyde Valley","A correspondent to Knowledge reports a black rain in the Clyde Valley, followed two days later by another. He notes the same valley had black rains on March 20 and 22, 1828.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Knowledge, 5-190","a black rain that fell in the Clyde Valley, March 1, 1884"),
(3,1876,"Falls","Meat falls from a clear sky","At Olympian Springs, Bath County, Kentucky, flakes of a substance that looks like beef fall from a clear sky onto a strip of ground about 100 by 50 yards. Reporters flock to the scene, and Prof. Lawrence Smith later calls it frog spawn or nostoc.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Scientific American, 34-197 and New York Times, March 10 & 12, 1876","Upon March 3, 1876, at Olympian Springs, Bath County, Kentucky, flakes of a substance that looked like beef"),
(5,1885,"Falls","A multi-ton object falls near Chico","According to the Chico Chronicle, a large object of very hard material, weighing several tons, falls from the sky near Chico, California, on the night of March 5-6. Fort cites the Monthly Weather Review.","NL","New Lands, citing Monthly Weather Review, March 1885","Upon the night of March 5-6, 1885"),
(6,1888,"Falls","Red rain over the Mediterranean","A red rain falls in the Mediterranean region and falls again twelve days later. When burned, the substance gives off a strong and persistent smell of animal matter.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing L'Astronomie, 1888-205","There was a red rain in the Mediterranean region, March 6, 1888"),
(7,1911,"Mysteries","Darkness at Louisville","At about 8 a.m., after moderate rain and hail, a storm of intense blackness spreads terror through Louisville, Kentucky, lasting about half an hour. Fort lists it among 'dark days' that he suspects had unexplained causes.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Weather Review, 32-522","At Louisville, Ky., March 7, 1911, at about 8 A.M."),
(9,1872,"Falls","A peculiar substance with the dust","A fall of dust said to be meteoric happens over three days. The Chemical News finds it to be a 'peculiar substance' of red iron ocher, carbonate of lime and organic matter.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Chemical News, 25-300","March 9, 10, 11, 1872"),
(12,1890,"Sky","A city in the sky over Ashland, Ohio","In the afternoon, the sky over Ashland, Ohio shows 'a large, unknown city'. Some thought it a mirage of the town of Mansfield. Fort cites the New York Sun.","NL","New Lands, citing New York Sun, March 16, 1890","in the afternoon of March 12th, in the sky of Ashland, Ohio"),
(13,1781,"Cosmos","A comet that was not","William Herschel reports 'a comet' that a year later is recognised by Lexell as a planet, Uranus. Fort argues the discovery was not the accident the textbooks claim.","NL","New Lands, citing Phil. Trans., 71-492","An account of a comet discovered on March 13, 1781"),
(14,1873,"Falls","Orange-red hail in Tuscany","Orange-red hail falls in Tuscany. Fort lists it among falls of coloured substances, along with a lavender-coloured rain at Oudon in 1903.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Notes and Queries, 9-5-16","Orange-red hail, March 14, 1873, in Tuscany"),
(15,1867,"Cosmos","The vanishing crater Linne","Dawes records 'an excessively minute black dot' in the middle of Linne, the lunar crater others had found whitened or changing. Fort adds it to his case that something was happening there.","NL","New Lands, citing Dawes","an excessively minute black dot in the middle of Linné"),
(16,1846,"Falls","Hairs fall at Shanghai","An olive-gray powder falls at Shanghai. Under the microscope it is an aggregate of hairs of two kinds, black ones and lighter ones. Fort places it near a fall of edible substance in Asia Minor.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Jour. Asiatic Soc. of Bengal, 1847","an olive-gray powder fell at Shanghai"),
(17,1669,"Falls","Putrid red fall at Chatillon","The records of the French Academy note that a reddish substance, 'thick, viscous, and putrid', fell at Chatillon-sur-Seine. Another red rain followed, in the same quarter of town.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing French Academy records","upon March 17, 1669, in the town of Chatillon-sur-Seine, fell a reddish substance"),
(18,1847,"Cosmos","Glowing spots on the dark Moon","On the nights of March 18 and 19, the Rev. T. Rankin and Prof. Chevalier see large luminous spots on the dark part of the Moon, and a general glow on its upper limb.","NL","New Lands, citing Rept. B. A., 1847-18","large luminous spots were seen upon the dark part of the moon"),
(19,1886,"Mysteries","Midnight at 3 p.m. in Oshkosh","At 3 p.m. darkness settles on Oshkosh, Wisconsin. In five minutes it is as dark as midnight. Fort cites the Monthly Weather Review and quotes the La Crosse Daily Republican.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Weather Review, March 1886","darkness suddenly settled upon the city of Oshkosh, Wis., at 3 P.M., March 19"),
(20,1862,"Cosmos","Lummis and the unknown planet","Lummis sees an unknown body of planetary size. This is the last in Le Verrier's list of six observations that seemed to agree on a planet inside Mercury's orbit.","BD","Book of the Damned, on Le Verrier's six observations","Lummis, March 20, 1862"),
(21,1877,"Cosmos","A brilliant light in Proclus","C. Barrett sees a brilliant illumination, 'not by the light of the sun', in the lunar crater Proclus. Fort adds it to a run of lunar lights seen that spring.","NL","New Lands, citing English Mechanic, 25-89","a brilliant illumination, and not by the light of the sun, according to C. Barrett"),
(22,1828,"Falls","Black rain in the Clyde Valley, twice","A black rain falls again in the Clyde Valley, two days after the first. Fort draws on a correspondent to Knowledge who lists these as recurrent.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Knowledge, 5-190","then again March 22, 1828"),
(23,1877,"Sky","Balls of fire over Vence","Dazzling balls of fire appear from a small cloud over Vence, France. They move slowly northward and are visible for more than an hour.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing L'Annee Scientifique, 1877-45","Lights that appeared in the sky, above Vence, France, March 23, 1877"),
(24,1840,"Falls","Grain falls at Rajkit","During a thunderstorm at Rajkit, India, a fall of grain occurs, reported by Col. Sykes to the British Association. The natives were excited because it was a kind unknown to them.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Amer. Jour. Sci., 1841-40","March 24, 1840--during a thunderstorm--at Rajkit, India, occurred a fall of grain"),
(25,1852,"Falls","Seeds fall on Prussia","On the night of March 24-25 a great quantity of seeds falls from the sky at Heinsberg, Erklenz and Juliers in Prussia. M. Schwann of Liege reported it to the Belgian Academy.","NL","New Lands, citing M. Schwann, Belgian Academy","Upon the night of March 24-25, 1852, a great quantity of seeds did fall from the sky"),
(26,1859,"Cosmos","Lescarbault's Vulcan","Dr. Lescarbault, an amateur astronomer of Orgeres, France, sees a body of planetary size cross the Sun. Le Verrier names it Vulcan. Fort followed the scandal that came after.","BD","Book of the Damned","he had seen a body of planetary size cross the sun"),
(27,1908,"Falls","White ash at Annoy","A white substance like ashes falls at Annoy, France. The Bulletin of the Astronomical Society of France calls it a 'curious phenomenon' and makes no attempt to trace it to a terrestrial source.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Bull. Soc. Astro. de France, 22-245","a white substance, like ashes, that fell at Annoy, France, March 27, 1908"),
(29,1905,"Sky","A beam of light over Cardiff","Several people near Cardiff see in the sky 'an appearance like a vertical beam of light, which was not due to a search light'. Arthur Mee reported it to the English Mechanic.","NL","New Lands, citing English Mechanic, 81-220","upon the night of March 29, 1905, seen in the sky"),
(30,1889,"Cosmos","A new black spot in Copernicus","Gaudibert sees a black spot for the first time near the centre of the lunar crater Copernicus. Fort notes it was the same day a new black spot was reported in Plinius.","NL","New Lands, citing L'Astronomie, 1890-235","a black spot that was seen for the first time, by Gaudibert, near the center of Copernicus"),
]
